* Connect to a database
@code bash
psql -h localhost -U username -d my_db
@end
The commands below run inside psql; names such as `users` are examples.

* List databases, connect, and inspect tables
@code sql
\l
\c my_db
\dn
\dt
\dt public.*
\d+ public.users
@end
`\dt` follows the search path; `\dt public.*` explicitly selects a schema.

* Expanded output and query timing
@code sql
\x auto
\timing on
SELECT * FROM public.users LIMIT 5;
@end
Automatic expanded output helps with rows too wide for the terminal.

* Inspect a change inside a transaction
@code sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE public.users SET name = 'Example' WHERE id = 1;
SELECT id, name FROM public.users WHERE id = 1;
ROLLBACK;
@end
Use `COMMIT;` instead of `ROLLBACK;` only when the change should persist. Keep transactions short: locks remain until they finish.

* Run a SQL file and stop on the first error
@code bash
psql -X -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -h localhost -U username -d my_db -f query.sql
@end
`-X` ignores local psql startup customizations. This alone does not undo statements committed before an error.

* Help and exit
@code sql
\?
\h SELECT
\q
@end

Related: {:postgres:}[PostgreSQL], {:import-export:}[backup and restore]. Reference: {https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-psql.html}[psql manual].
